## 2. Part 1 — Jobs to Be Done

---

### STATEMENT — Part intro
- Kicker: Part 1
- Title: JOBS TO BE DONE
- On-slide: nothing else
- Speaker notes: —
- 🎨 Visual hint: Full-bleed --sienna background. "Part 1" small muted text bottom-left. "JOBS TO BE DONE" in large bold display type. No other content.

---

### STATEMENT — The core claim
- Kicker: Part 1 · Reframe
- Title: People don't buy\nproducts. They *hire*\nthem for a job.
- On-slide: Caption only — "Different people. Same job."
- Speaker notes: A 25-year-old and a 55-year-old can hire the exact same product for the exact same job. Two people who look identical on a persona slide can be hiring completely different jobs. JTBD explains motivation — not who someone is, but what they're trying to get done, and why they reached for a solution to do it.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Two silhouette icons, visually different (age/style), both with an arrow pointing to the same central "job" icon (a target or checkmark in --sienna).

---

### NUMBERED — Three job types
- Kicker: Part 1 · Framework
- Title: Every job carries\nthree layers.
- On-slide: 3 labels only, one short example each:
  - **Functional** — "Get this task done."
  - **Emotional** — "Feel confident, not anxious."
  - **Social** — "Be seen as in control."
- Speaker notes: Most real jobs are functional, emotional, and social all at once. A functional-only reading usually misses why the friction actually hurts. Full examples to walk through live: functional — "Get my application approved without re-submitting the same document"; emotional — "Feel confident I haven't missed a step"; social — "Be seen as someone who has this under control, not someone overwhelmed by paperwork." Full framework file: `Library/frameworks/jtbd/framework-jtbd.md`.
- 🎨 Visual hint: 3 stacked cards, each with an icon (task/checkbox for functional, heart/pulse for emotional, two-people for social) + bold label + short on-slide phrase.

---

### STATEMENT — The job statement formula
- Kicker: Part 1 · Write it right
- Title: Start with the\n*situation*, not\nthe person.
- On-slide: Formula only — *"When [situation], I want to [motivation], so I can [expected outcome]."*
- Speaker notes: Circumstance-first, not persona-first — that's what separates a job statement from a persona line. Weak example: "As a busy professional, I want a fast checkout." Strong example: "When I'm applying for a home loan while managing a property settlement deadline, I want to know exactly what's outstanding at a glance, so I can stop re-checking the portal daily out of anxiety I've missed something." Point out live: the weak one opens with "as a," the strong one opens with "when."
- 🎨 Visual hint: One large formula card, --sienna border, each bracket term ([situation] etc.) colour-coded distinctly. No weak/strong example cards on-slide — deliver the contrast verbally from notes.

---

### NUMBERED — JTBD vs persona
- Kicker: Part 1 · The trap
- Title: Persona answers who.\nJob answers *why,\nright now.*
- On-slide: Two-column labels only:
  - **Persona** — demographics, stable across products
  - **JTBD** — circumstance, stable only for this job
- Speaker notes: Quick test to give the room: does your statement start with "when [situation]" or does it secretly start with "as a [type of person]"? The second one is a persona in disguise. Same person can hire different jobs in different circumstances — that's the whole point of circumstance over identity.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Two columns side by side, persona (--ink-ghost, muted) vs JTBD (--sienna). No footer text on-slide — deliver the quick test verbally.

---

### DIAGRAM — Break the job into its own stages
- Kicker: Part 1 · What it looks like in action
- Title: The job says *why.*\nStages say *where.*
- On-slide: generic placeholder chain only, no real labels — Stage 1 → Stage 2 → Stage 3 → ⋯ → Stage N — plus one small standing label underneath: "Your stages, your words — usually 3 to 6"
- Speaker notes: A job statement explains motivation. It doesn't say where in the process that motivation shows up, or how it shifts along the way — and a recommendation is hard to scope without knowing which stage it belongs to. There's no fixed universal vocabulary here — say that explicitly, since a Job Map with fixed step names exists in the wild (Ulwick's Outcome-Driven Innovation, cited in the framework file) and this session deliberately trades that fixed scaffold for one that flexes per project. Walk the applied example live from the framework file: the home-loan job broken into 4 stages — realising I need a loan, gathering documents, submitting and waiting, approval and settlement — each with its own job statement. The actual job derived earlier, "let me stop relying on my memory," sits specifically at stage 3, submitting and waiting, not spread across the whole thing. That's the payoff: the statement says it matters, the stage breakdown says exactly where. Full detail and the worked table: `Library/frameworks/jtbd/framework-jtbd.md`.
- 🎨 Visual hint: A generic N-node horizontal chain, dashed/lighter styling to signal "placeholder, not a fixed template" — 4-5 nodes shown with a "..." ellipsis node before the final one to imply flexible count. Node labels read "Stage 1," "Stage 2," etc., not real step names. Small muted caption beneath: "Your stages, your words." Deliberately looser and less "finished" than a fixed diagram would be, so it doesn't read as a scaffold to memorise.

---

### STATEMENT — Hypothesis, not finding
- Kicker: Part 1 · Say this out loud
- Title: "Until it's tested,\nit's a *hypothesis* —\nnot a job."
- On-slide: small label under the quote — "candidate job hypothesis"
- Speaker notes: A job statement written before talking to anyone has the same evidence status as an unproven entry on the Assumption Map. JTBD sounds authoritative when it's well-formatted — that confidence is easy to mistake for evidence. Label it a candidate job hypothesis until a pattern from real interviews confirms it. This is the single most important framing line of the session — say it slowly.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Dark --ink full-bleed background. Bold quote in --ochre display type.

---

### MILESTONE — Build Your Stage Map
- Kicker: Activity · Part 1
- Num: 9
- Title: Minutes
- On-slide: nothing beyond kicker/num/title
- Speaker notes: Name the stages your project's job actually goes through, in your own words — usually 3 to 6, no fixed vocabulary. Point your AI tool at your audit findings and Assumption Map, already in your project folder, no need to re-explain the project. Ask it to cross-check both files and draft a candidate JTBD hypothesis for each stage, and to say which existing file supports each one. Check each against the persona trap. Deliverable: `jtbd-map.md` — one hypothesis per stage, labelled draft.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Full-bleed --sienna moment, matching Audit deck's milestone style.

