## 3. Part 2 — Stakeholder Check-In

---

### STATEMENT — Part intro
- Kicker: Part 2
- Title: STAKEHOLDER CHECK-IN
- On-slide: nothing else
- Speaker notes: —
- 🎨 Visual hint: Full-bleed --sienna background. "Part 2" small muted text bottom-left. "STAKEHOLDER CHECK-IN" in large bold display type.

---

### STATEMENT — Not a new method
- Kicker: Part 2 · Reuse, don't reinvent
- Title: This is your\nAssumption Map —\naimed at *one question.*
- On-slide: the one question only — "What job does the business believe the customer is hiring us for?"
- Speaker notes: The switch-interview technique reconstructs a real customer's decision journey. A stakeholder never switched to anything — running that technique on them is a category error, not a shorter version of the same thing. What this conversation actually produces is an assumption. Capture it the way the mentee already knows how: WHO/WHAT/WHY/SIGNAL, same matrix as last session.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Reuse the same 2×2 Assumption Map matrix visual from the Audit & Desk Research deck, faded/ghosted, with a single new sticky note added in the top-left labelled "Assumed customer job." Signals continuity, not a new framework.

---

### DIAGRAM — Place the guess on the map
- Kicker: Part 2 · Before the real meeting
- Title: Guess first.\nThen go check it.
- On-slide: the same generic stage chain from Part 1, one node marked with a small "?" tag, no real labels
- Speaker notes: Before the real check-in happens, place a provisional guess of the stakeholder's belief onto the stage map from Part 1 — which stage do they likely think the job matters most at, and does that match the hypotheses on the map? This isn't the confirmed answer, it's a rehearsal: a specific gap to go test instead of walking in with an open-ended "what do you think the job is." Whichever stage shows the biggest gap between guess and hypothesis is the one to listen for specifically once the real conversation happens.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Same dashed placeholder stage chain from Part 1, now with one node circled in --sienna and a small "?" badge, signalling "unconfirmed guess," not a finished answer.

---

### MILESTONE — Place the Stakeholder's Assumed Job on the Map
- Kicker: Activity · Part 2
- Num: 5
- Title: Minutes
- On-slide: nothing beyond kicker/num/title
- Speaker notes: For each stage on the map, write your best guess at what the stakeholder currently believes the job is there — provisional, before the real check-in. Mark where that guess matches your own hypothesis and where it diverges. Circle the stage with the biggest gap. Deliverable: assumed-job placement added to `jtbd-map.md`, labelled draft, pending the real check-in.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Full-bleed --sienna moment.

---

### NUMBERED — Questions to bring
- Kicker: Part 2 · In the room
- Title: Three questions.\nOne meeting you\nalready have.
- On-slide: 3 short prompts only:
  1. "What job do you think the customer is doing here?"
  2. "Why do you believe that?"
  3. "What would change your mind?"
- Speaker notes: Aim these at the stage that showed the biggest gap in the previous activity, not the whole job in the abstract. Fold this into a 1:1, a standup, a check-in already on the calendar — don't book a new meeting. Getting dedicated time with a stakeholder is often the harder scheduling problem in this whole assignment, and it's easy to underestimate that if you've never tried to book a PM or business owner's calendar for something that isn't urgent to them.
- 🎨 Visual hint: 3 numbered question cards in a row. Small calendar-with-checkmark icon below, no caption text on-slide.

---

### MILESTONE — Prepare Your Stakeholder Check-in
- Kicker: Activity · Part 2
- Num: 4
- Title: Minutes
- On-slide: nothing beyond kicker/num/title
- Speaker notes: Identify the meeting already on your calendar this attaches to. Draft your 2–3 questions, aimed at the stage with the biggest gap. Add them as a new entry on your existing `assumption-map.md` — same formula, same matrix — ready to log the real answer once the meeting happens. Deliverable: the new entry logged in `assumption-map.md`.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Full-bleed --sienna moment.

