## 5. Part 4 — Actionable Insight

---

### STATEMENT — Part intro
- Kicker: Part 4
- Title: ACTIONABLE INSIGHT
- On-slide: nothing else
- Speaker notes: —
- 🎨 Visual hint: Full-bleed --sienna background. "Part 4" small muted text bottom-left. "ACTIONABLE INSIGHT" in large bold display type.

---

### DIAGRAM — Assumed vs actual, same map
- Kicker: Part 4 · The gap
- Title: The business\nbelieved X. The\nevidence says Y.
- On-slide: two card labels only — "Assumed job" / "Actual job"
- Speaker notes: This is the moment the two things built earlier in the session come together on the same map: the stakeholder's assumed job, now confirmed for real in the check-in, against the actual job derived from your interview pattern. Comparing them, stage by stage, is the strongest insight-generating move in this session — specific, evidence-backed, and hard to dismiss as opinion. Walk through the example from the framework doc live if there's time: assumed "show me my status" vs actual "let me stop relying on my memory," located at stage 3 — submitting and waiting.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Two stacked cards, both anchored on the same small stage-chain strip from Part 1/Part 2 so the room sees it's the same map. TOP card (--ink-ghost, muted): "Assumed job." BOTTOM card (--sienna border): "Actual job," small stage-number tag in the corner. Bold "≠" symbol between them, large and central. No statement text inside the cards on-slide — deliver the specific example verbally.

---

### NUMBERED — The insight formula, three legs
- Kicker: Part 4 · Write it right
- Title: Two legs isn't\nenough anymore.
- On-slide: 3 block labels only — Observation / Implication / Recommended decision
- Speaker notes: Audit & Desk Research taught pattern → assumption confirmed/broken → why it matters. That's two legs. A senior/lead insight needs a third: what decision this unlocks. Weak example: "it matters because it's causing repeat support calls." Strong example adds: "We should prototype a proactive confirmation instead of iterating further on the status page." An insight that stops at "why it matters" still leaves the stakeholder to figure out what to do about it.
- 🎨 Visual hint: 3 connected blocks left to right, arrows between. Block 3 visually heavier/bolder (--sienna fill) than blocks 1–2 (outline only) to signal it's the new, previously-missing piece. No example text on the blocks — labels only.

---

### STATEMENT — The gut-check test
- Kicker: Part 4 · Before you present it
- Title: "Would this change\na decision?"
- On-slide: quote only
- Speaker notes: For every insight drafted, ask: would a stakeholder change what they do next because of this sentence? If the honest answer is no, the recommended decision isn't specific enough yet — go back and name one.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Dark --ink full-bleed background. Bold quote in --ochre display type, centred.

