## 6. Part 5 — The Stakeholder Pitch

---

### STATEMENT — Part intro
- Kicker: Part 5
- Title: THE STAKEHOLDER PITCH
- On-slide: nothing else
- Speaker notes: —
- 🎨 Visual hint: Full-bleed --sienna background. "Part 5" small muted text bottom-left. "THE STAKEHOLDER PITCH" in large bold display type.

---

### NUMBERED — Observation vs implication
- Kicker: Part 5 · Lead with this
- Title: Data opens the\nappendix. Implication\nopens the pitch.
- On-slide: structure only — Implication headline → evidence → recommended decision
- Speaker notes: Observation is what happened. Implication is what it means and why the person in the room should care. A senior/lead insight leads with implication. Weaker, leads with observation: "3 out of 3 users check their status page daily, sometimes more than once." Stronger, leads with implication: "We're asking users to do our job for us — checking daily because they don't trust us to tell them when something changes. That's driving our support call volume, and it's fixable." Full notes and quotes go in the appendix, not the core pitch.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Simple 3-step horizontal flow (headline → evidence → decision). No weak/strong example cards on-slide — deliver the contrast verbally.

---

### MILESTONE — Draft Your Stakeholder Pitch
- Kicker: Activity · Part 5
- Num: 7
- Title: Minutes
- On-slide: nothing beyond kicker/num/title
- Speaker notes: Take one real insight from your Audit & Desk Research readout — not a hypothetical. Rewrite the headline to lead with implication. Add the recommended-decision line if it's missing. Solo: run the "would this change a decision" test on yourself, optionally cross-check with AI against your saved readout and audit insights. Pair path, if a partner's here: deliver your 60-second pitch, they push back with "that's just your opinion" — point to your evidence. Deliverable: updated `stakeholder-readout-template.md`, plus a rendered HTML one-pager if presenting live.
- 🎨 Visual hint: Full-bleed --sienna moment.

---

## 7. Assignment + Close

---

### PRACTICE — Assignment: From Assumed Job to Actionable Insight
- Kicker: Assignment
- Title: Test the Job.\nFind the Gap.
- On-slide: 7 short step labels only, no full sentences:
  - Run the stakeholder check-in
  - Conduct 2–3 customer interviews
  - Derive the actual job
  - Update the stage breakdown from real findings
  - Update `jtbd-map.md`
  - Write the insight (3 legs)
  - Update the stakeholder readout
- Speaker notes: Full detail per step — run the stakeholder check-in inside the meeting already on the calendar, log the assumed job on the Assumption Map. Conduct 2–3 JTBD-informed customer interviews, not one. After each, cross-check it against the hypothesis (AI already has the files, no re-pasting needed). Derive the actual job from what repeats. Update the draft stage breakdown from this session with what the real interviews actually showed — confirm, revise, or add stages. Update `jtbd-map.md`: hypothesis / assumed / actual / gap / linked insight / confirmed stage breakdown. Write the insight using the 3-leg formula, scoped to its stage, into the Insight Synthesis Template. Update the Stakeholder Readout draft with the implication-first headline. AI prompt to mention: point AI at the 3 saved interview files and the hypothesis file, ask it to cross-check the pattern and draft the actual job statement. Deliverable: updated `jtbd-map.md`, Insight Synthesis Template, and Stakeholder Readout Template (all markdown), plus a rendered HTML one-pager of the readout for the actual presentation.
- 🎨 Visual hint: 6 short numbered labels in a vertical list, no body text beneath each. `jtbd-map.md` schema preview (5 columns) as a small table thumbnail on the right.

---

### END — Closing
- Kicker: One thing to carry forward
- Title: "The job is what\nrepeats. The insight\nis what *moves\nsomeone.*"
- On-slide: title + sign only
- Speaker notes: A candidate hypothesis becomes a job when a pattern confirms it. An observation becomes an insight when it names a decision. Neither one is finished until it's been tested against something outside your own head.
- Sign: — Winnie Nguyen
